stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Requested: CRM raises Cancellation Request\n(reason, date, financial_implications flag)

    note left of Requested
        v1.3.2: approval sequence below is CONFIGURATION-DRIVEN — read
        from the CANCELLATION workflow definition's active version
        (§1.6, §1.9), same engine as ATS/Sale Deed. This diagram shows
        the DEFAULT configuration a fresh install ships with, not
        hard-coded application logic.
    end note

    Requested --> CFO_Pending: financial_implications = true\n(default config: CFO stage applicable_if_financial_implications = TRUE)
    Requested --> Mgmt_Pending: financial_implications = false\n(default config: CFO stage skipped, same pattern as Direct Sale Deed skipping ATS)

    CFO_Pending --> Mgmt_Pending: CFO Approves
    CFO_Pending --> Requested: CFO Sends Back (remark mandatory)

    Mgmt_Pending --> Approved: Management Approves
    Mgmt_Pending --> Requested: Management Sends Back (remark mandatory)

    Approved --> [*]: Transaction.lifecycle_status → CANCELLED

    note right of Approved
        On approval (via recompute_unit_status, §1.3b — one
        authoritative function, not scattered app-layer logic):
        - transaction.lifecycle_status = CANCELLED
        - units.status recomputed → CANCELLED
          (partial unique index on transactions.unit_id
           WHERE lifecycle_status = 'ACTIVE' no longer
           blocks a new transaction against this unit)
        - the cancelled transaction is NEVER deleted —
          full workflow_actions / documents / financial
          history stays permanently queryable, unmodified
        - a later transaction (rebooking) may be created
          against the same unit; it is a fully separate row,
          created via the NORMAL Allocation process, with its
          OWN independent financial snapshot — never copied
          from this one. See workflow-cancellation-rebooking
          note and §1.9c/§1.9d for the full rule.
    end note

